# Prompt history tick test: the 60 s CI timeout did not need a slow daemon (2026-10-04)

Two CI runs on 2026-10-04 failed one test each, the same test, on commits that changed
nothing near it. The comment inside the test blamed the machine ("CI runners can starve the
history-search daemon thread under concurrent workspace tests") and its remedy was a 60
second deadline. The deadline is the wrong remedy, because the interleaving that produces
the failure needs no starvation: it is the daemon answering *quickly*.

== 0. What the two runs said

    $ gh run list -L 8 --json databaseId,headSha,conclusion \
          --jq '.[]|"\(.databaseId) \(.headSha[0:8]) \(.conclusion)"'

    37194865831 c8104865
    37184965394 36d0809e failure
    37183474313 0faeee1d failure

Both failures are the same test in the same job, and every other job of both runs was green.
The `rust` job of run 37184965394:

    test app::app_view::tests::needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery
    has been running for over 60 seconds
    test app::app_view::tests::needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery ... FAILED

    ---- app::app_view::tests::needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery stdout ----

    thread 'app::app_view::tests::needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery'
    (79492) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs:669:5:
    tick() must poll the history daemon and deliver results

    test result: FAILED. 9199 passed; 1 failed; 25 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out;
    finished in 62.16s

Run 37183474313 is the same panic at the same line, `finished in 62.93s`. Nothing else in
either run failed; the pager lib totals (9199 passed / 1 failed / 25 ignored) are identical.

== 1. The test asked for two things to happen on the same tick

The loop it failed in was:

    while std::time::Instant::now() < deadline {
        if app.tick() && app.agents[&id].prompt.history_search.result_count() == 2 {
            delivered = true;
            break;
        }
        std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(1));
    }

`app.tick()` does not mean "the overlay is open". `needs_animation()` is the predicate that
means that -- it is `tick_demand() != TickDemand::None`, and its list of reasons does include
`agent.prompt.history_search.is_active()`
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view.rs:5814`). `tick()` is something else: it
accumulates `needs_redraw` from every state change it can see
and returns that, and the event loop requests a draw on the animation arm only when
`tick()` says yes (`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/event_loop.rs:2787`). So `tick()`
returning `true` is a *change signal*, and the only history-related source of one is:

    needs_redraw |= agent.prompt.history_search.poll();

(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view.rs:5521`, the only production call site of
`HistorySearchState::poll`). `poll()` reports a change exactly once per generation:

    if snap.generation == self.last_gen {
        return false;
    }
    self.last_gen = snap.generation;

(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs:490`).

The other half is `activate()`. `activate_inner` pushes the history to the daemon and then
reads the shared snapshot for itself, adopting whatever generation it finds as `last_gen`:

    self.refresh_items(history);
    // Eagerly grab the initial snapshot
    if let Some(daemon) = &self.daemon {
        self.snapshot = daemon.shared.lock()
            .unwrap_or_else(std::sync::PoisonError::into_inner).clone();
    }
    self.last_gen = self.snapshot.generation;

(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs:431`, abbreviated; the
publisher writes under the same mutex at `:200`,
`*out.lock().unwrap() = Snapshot { items, generation }`).

Put together: if the daemon publishes generation N *after* `refresh_items` sent the items but
*before* the eager grab read them, then `activate()` returns with the two results already in
`self.snapshot` and `last_gen` already equal to N. From then on `poll()` finds no change on
any tick, so `tick()` reports no change, and the conjunction above can never be satisfied --
not after 60 seconds, not ever. The count is already right; the redraw flag is what never
arrives. The failure is a test asserting a delivery that had already happened.

== 2. Negative result: a pause after activate() does not reproduce it

The obvious first probe was to slow the *test* down, and it came back green. With
`std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(400))` inserted between `activate()` and the loop,
the committed body still passed, in 0.41 s. That is not a contradiction of section 1: sleeping
after `activate()` cannot move the publish into a window that `activate()` has already left.
The publish lands after the grab, the next `tick()` picks up the new generation, and both
halves of the conjunction are true on that iteration.

== 3. The interleaving that does reproduce it, forced

The window is between the `send` and the read, so the pause goes there, inside the product
(`views/history_search.rs`, after `self.send(Msg::SetItems(items));` in `refresh_items`), for
one measurement only. The deadline was shortened to 3 s so the experiment would not burn a
minute per round; with the shipped 60 s deadline the same run fails at 60 s, which is what the
two CI runs show.

    === E1: committed body, daemon publishes inside the eager grab ===
    [e1-old] rc=101 -> FAIL
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 3.21s
        tick() must poll the history daemon and deliver results

Same injected pause, rewritten body:

    === E2: fixed body, same ordering ===
    [e2-fixed] rc=0 -> PASS
        test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 0.21s

    restore crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs: byte-identical
    restore crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs: byte-identical

== 4. What the rewritten test asserts

Delivery and the redraw it reports are now observed separately, and the strong claim moved to
a query that cannot have been polled before it was sent:

    let before = app.agents[&id].prompt.history_search.result_count();
    ... update_query("second") ...
    while std::time::Instant::now() < deadline {
        let reported_redraw = app.tick();
        let count = app.agents[&id].prompt.history_search.result_count();
        if count != before {
            delivery_reported_redraw = reported_redraw;
        }
        narrowed = count == 1;
        if narrowed { break; }
        std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(1));
    }

`result_count()` only moves inside `poll()`, and in production `poll()` only runs inside
`tick()`, so "the count changed" is a delivery by `tick()` by construction, and it is captured
on the same iteration that observed it. The shipped code is unchanged by this batch: nothing
in it was wrong. `activate()` is meant to grab a snapshot -- the comment on `refresh_items`
says "activate re-sends the items it is given, so nothing is lost" -- and the event that opens
the overlay draws through the input arm (`event_loop.rs:2707`, `result.needs_draw`), so a
result set that arrived during activation reaches the screen without owing a change flag to
any later tick.

== 5. Non-vacuity: two mutants of the shipped tick()

Both mutants are injected into `app_view.rs`, run, and reverted with plain `cp` followed by
`cmp`. The baseline and the restore are the same bytes.

M1 deletes the poll line from `tick()`:

    [m1-no-poll] rc=101
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 60.01s
        the overlay must have the activated results, from activate() or from a later tick

This also settles the direction of section 1's mechanism on an ordinary machine: with no poll
at all, the count never reaches 2, so in a normal (unmutated) run the results do arrive
through a `tick()`-driven poll rather than through the eager grab.

M2 keeps the call and throws its result away, so state still updates and nothing reports it:

    [m2] rc=101
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 0.01s
        thread 'app::app_view::tests::needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery'
        (2955471) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs:707:5:
        the tick that delivers new results has to report a redraw, or the overlay never repaints

    restore app_view.rs: byte-identical

M2 is the mutant that matters for this batch: it delivers every result and reports none of
them, and only the second assertion sees it. M1 is caught by the first one, so both halves of
the rewritten test are load-bearing.

Note what M1 also rules out: with no poll in `tick()` at all, the count never reaches 2 within
60 s, so the eager grab is not usually what satisfies the first loop. The eager grab can
satisfy it (section 3's forced interleaving), and that is precisely why the first loop may not
insist on a redraw; on an unloaded machine it is `tick()` that delivers, and the first loop
therefore still exercises the shipped path.

== 6. Stability and formatting

The rewritten test five times in a row:

    [repeat-1] rc=0
        test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 0.01s
    (… 3 runs elided here, all rc=0 and the same line)
    [repeat-5] rc=0
        test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 9224 filtered out;
        finished in 0.01s

    $ cargo fmt -p xai-grok-pager -- --check

Both formatter checks are silent on the tree as committed:

    $ cargo fmt -p xai-grok-pager -- --check

    $ cargo fmt --all -- --check

(no output from either; `fmt rc=0`)

The whole `app::app_view` module, and then the pager lib, against a freshly built tree:

    $ touch crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view.rs \
          crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs
    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-pager --lib app::app_view
    test result: ok. 260 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 8965 filtered out;
    finished in 0.09s

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-pager --lib
    test result: ok. 9200 passed; 0 failed; 25 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out;
    finished in 7.05s

The `touch` is not decoration. The sweep run right after the M2 restore reported
`259 passed; 1 failed` with M2's own message at line 707, which looked like a second failure
introduced by the fix. It was the mutant binary: the restore copies with `cp -p`, so the
restored file carries the saved copy's mtime, which is older than the artifact cargo had just
built for the mutant, and cargo calls that fresh. Any cargo result after a restore is a
measurement of the previous source unless the mtime is moved. The numbers above are from a run
where `cmp` had confirmed the bytes and `touch` had confirmed the build.

Neither side of the race is this fork's. `git log -S "self.last_gen =
self.snapshot.generation"` on `views/history_search.rs` lands on `c68e39f6` (the initial
open-source publish), and `git log -S "needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery"`
on the test file lands on `e5fd4816`. The two have been able to lose this race since the
first commit; what changed is
that CI now runs the pager lib often enough to notice.

== 7. Reproduce it

`c8104865` is the last commit whose `app_view_tests.rs` still holds the pre-fix body, so the
recipe names that commit rather than a moving `HEAD^`.

    $ cp crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs /var/tmp/keep-history.rs
    $ cp crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs /var/tmp/keep-tests.rs
    $ git show "c8104865:crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs" \
          > crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs
    $ sed -i '/self\.send(Msg::SetItems(items));/a\        std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(200));' \
          crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs
    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-pager --lib needs_animation_gates_prompt_history_tick_delivery
    $ cp /var/tmp/keep-history.rs crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs
    $ cp /var/tmp/keep-tests.rs crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs
    $ cmp /var/tmp/keep-history.rs crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/history_search.rs
    $ cmp /var/tmp/keep-tests.rs crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/app/app_view_tests.rs

The `cargo test` line fails with `tick() must poll the history daemon and deliver results`
after roughly 60 seconds, which is the CI failure; the two `cmp` lines print nothing. Without
the injected pause the pre-fix body passes, so the recipe above is a reproduction of the
interleaving, not of a machine under load.
